There is a very easy and intuitive way to also solve this in O(n) time and O(1) memory without having to go backwards. A bit longer code, but may be easier to understand algorithmically.
Consider that all values are either positive (meaning we can always move right by at least 1 step) or 0 (so in this cell we get stuck). So we go value by value, at each value we calculate the furthest point we can jump to and keep updating this furthest point as we go. If we hit a zero - we just check if our furthest point so far is beyond that zero. If yes - we know we won't get stuck at this 0 as we can jump over it from one of the previous cells, so we go to the next cell and continue with the algorithm. If the furthest point is at the same point as the 0 we found - then there is no way for us to proceed from either this 0 or any point before it, so we just immediately return False. It works in O(n) time and we only use a single additional variable of constant size for the current furthest reachable point.

Here's an example in Python:

class Solution:
    def canJump(self, nums: List[int]) -> bool:
        if nums == [0]:
            return True
        longest_reach = 0
        for i in range(len(nums)):
            # Check if we can reach further than a zero we have found from one of the previous cells
            if nums[i] == 0:
                if i == len(nums) - 1:
                    return True
                if longest_reach > i:
                    continue
                else:
                    return False
            # Update the longest reachable point if we can reach even further from the current cell
            reach = i + nums[i]
            if reach > longest_reach:
                longest_reach = reach
        return True

